---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import SectionTitle from '../../components/SectionTitle.astro';
import CaseFeature from '../../components/CaseFeature.astro';
import GridLines from '../../components/GridLines.astro';
import Pic from '../../components/Pic.astro';

const img = '/images/case-hirepeak';
---

<CaseLayout
  title="HirePeak — Alex Abrosov"
  description="An AI-powered HRM system that analyzes and enhances candidate CVs, helping recruiters streamline hiring."
  accent="var(--color-accent-gold)"
>
  <!-- Start screen -->
  <div class="case-opening">
    <section class="case-hero container case-grid">
      <div class="case-hero-left">
        <div class="case-hero-text">
          <p class="t-subtitle" data-reveal>2026</p>
          <h1 class="case-hero-title t-h1" data-reveal>Hire<em>Peak</em></h1>
          <p class="case-hero-intro t-body-2" data-reveal>
            HirePeak is an advanced HRM system that streamlines the hiring process by collecting,
            analyzing, and enhancing candidate CVs using AI insights. It helps job seekers refine their
            resumes to meet market standards while providing recruiters with structured feedback and
            optimization tools for a more efficient interview process.
          </p>
        </div>

        <dl class="case-meta" data-reveal>
          <div>
            <div class="case-meta-group">
              <dt class="t-subtitle">Client</dt>
              <dd class="t-body">Confidential Global Enterprise</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="case-meta-group">
              <dt class="t-subtitle">Industry</dt>
              <dd class="t-body">Talent Acquisition</dd>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="t-subtitle">Services</dt>
            <dd class="t-body">
              Discovery &amp; Research<br />Ideation &amp; Features Dev<br />Rebranding<br />Design System
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="t-subtitle">Responsibilities</dt>
            <dd class="t-body">Product Design<br />Frontend<br />Management</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class="case-tile case-hero-tile" data-reveal>
        <Pic eager class="plate" src={`${img}/hero.jpg`} alt="HirePeak documents workspace, notepad and key positions" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="case-screen">
      <Pic class="plate" src={`${img}/screen.jpg`} alt="HirePeak running on a laptop" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Challenge -->
  <section class="case-section container case-grid challenge">
    <SectionTitle title="Challenge" />
    <p class="col-wide case-intro t-body-2" data-reveal>
      Hiring stalls on both sides of the same document. Job seekers send out resumes they cannot tell
      are wrong for the role, and recruiters read hundreds of them with no consistent way to compare
      candidates or explain a decision. The task was to build an AI hiring tool that gives both sides
      structured, actionable insight: a resume the candidate can fix before applying, and an evaluation
      the recruiter can act on and respond to within the day. The harder part was calibration. The AI
      had to sharpen the decision, not make it, so recruiters stay in control instead of being led by
      automation.
    </p>
    <ol class="col-wide case-goals" data-reveal>
      <li>
        <p class="t-h3">1.</p>
        <p class="t-body">
          Inconsistent resume quality. Job seekers struggle to tailor their resumes to a role, which
          leads to mismatched applications and lower chances of success.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="t-h3">2.</p>
        <p class="t-body">
          Inefficient candidate evaluation. Recruiters have no fast way to assess resumes and return
          actionable feedback, so the whole hiring process drags.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="t-h3">3.</p>
        <p class="t-body">
          Balancing AI and human input. Insights have to stay actionable rather than overwhelming, so
          users make informed decisions without feeling overpowered by automation.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="case-tile case-challenge-tile" data-reveal>
      <span class="badge"><img src={`${img}/badge.svg`} alt="Client mark" width="88" height="88" /></span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Solution -->
  <section class="case-section container case-grid solution">
    <SectionTitle title="Solution" />
    <div class="col-wide case-solution-intro" data-reveal>
      <p class="t-body-2">
        I designed the product around the CV itself. A recruiter uploads a candidate&rsquo;s file, tags
        the target positions and level, and the AI rewrites it to the market standard with every change
        highlighted and explained. The decision I argued for most was keeping the recruiter in charge:
        each suggestion can be accepted, edited or rejected, and the Documents hub holds the whole
        pipeline, its statuses and notes, in one place.
      </p>
      <p class="case-tags t-button">
        <span>CV Generator</span><i></i><span>Dashboards</span><i></i><span>Market Recommendations</span><i></i>
        <span>Documents Hub</span><i></i><span>Notes &amp; Support</span><i></i><span>and more</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="case-label col-label t-subtitle feature-label" data-reveal>/ Feature 1</p>
    <div class="col-text feature-one" data-reveal>
      <div class="case-block">
        <h3 class="t-subtitle" data-label="Feature 1">CV Generator</h3>
        <p class="t-body">
          The core flow is four steps: upload the CV, select the key positions, optimize it to the
          market and export. It accepts PDF, DOC and XLS files, links and cloud imports, and each step
          unlocks only when the previous one is complete, so a recruiter can leave the flow and pick it
          up later without losing progress.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="case-tile case-solution-tile" data-reveal>
      <Pic class="plate" src={`${img}/solution.jpg`} alt="CV generator flow" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Solution details -->
  <div class="feature-stack">
    <CaseFeature
      index={2}
      title="Market Recommendations"
      body="Each generated CV comes with a scored list of recommendations and the top skills for the target position in the current year. Changes are highlighted in place; the recruiter applies them all at once, accepts them one by one, or edits the text directly."
      image={`${img}/feature-2.jpg`}
      alt="Scored market recommendations on a generated CV"
    />
    <CaseFeature
      index={3}
      title="Export & Sharing"
      body="The finished CV is exported as a PDF in the firm's template, ready to download or share with hiring managers. Restarting the flow keeps the selected positions, so a second version for another role takes minutes rather than a morning."
      image={`${img}/feature-3.jpg`}
      alt="Exporting the finished CV as a PDF"
    />
    <CaseFeature
      index={4}
      title="Documents Hub"
      body="Every CV lives in one list with its owner, date, status and notes, filtered by created, shared and archived. Recent files, favourites and the team wiki sit beside it, so onboarding and process guides are always one click away."
      image={`${img}/feature-4.jpg`}
      alt="Documents hub listing every CV with its status"
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Results -->
  <section class="case-section container case-grid case-results results">
    <GridLines local />
    <SectionTitle title="Results" />
    <p class="col-wide case-intro t-body-2" data-reveal>
      After the rollout, recruiters got structured, actionable insight on every CV, and a place to keep
      that knowledge and their team practices inside the tool rather than in private notes. The AI works
      as a partner; the final call stays with the person. Two quarters after the production release, the
      tool became the standard for CV preparation across every area of the company&rsquo;s recruiting.
    </p>
    <ol class="col-wide case-outcomes outcomes-3" data-reveal>
      <li>
        <p class="t-h3">1.</p>
        <h3 class="t-subtitle">CV preparation time cut by 41%</h3>
        <p class="t-body">
          A CV ready for the market now takes under twenty minutes instead of the better part of a
          morning, across the whole talent team.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="t-h3">2.</p>
        <h3 class="t-subtitle">87% of suggestions accepted</h3>
        <p class="t-body">
          On average recruiters override one suggestion in eight and edit it by hand, which is the
          balance of AI and human judgement the product was designed for.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="t-h3">3.</p>
        <h3 class="t-subtitle">Adopted across four practice areas</h3>
        <p class="t-body">
          The pilot started with accounting roles; the position library and CV standards now cover
          audit, tax, advisory and consulting hiring.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Section heights come from the Figma frame; everything else is the shared
     case rhythm in case.css, so this page resizes with the other four. */
  .challenge {
    min-height: 1197px;
  }

  .solution {
    min-height: 1595px;
  }

  .results {
    min-height: 835px;
  }

  /* ---- Finished tiles ----
     These come out of Figma whole - the accent, the mockups and the spacing
     between them are already in the file - so each one covers its tile rather
     than being placed on top of it. */
  .case-hero-tile :global(.plate),
  .case-solution-tile :global(.plate),
  .case-screen :global(.plate) {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    transform: none;
    border-radius: inherit;
  }

  /* The tile keeps the height case.css gives it, tied to the pinned start
     screen, so it can never grow past that section and show under the screen
     sliding over it. The plate sits inside rather than filling: it is taller
     than the tile is, and its own margin is the same accent as the tile, so
     what is left over reads as part of the panel. */
  .case-hero-tile :global(.plate) {
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  .feature-label,
  .feature-one {
    margin-top: 140px;
  }

  /* The mark is vector, so it stays crisp however large the tile gets; it
     measures 88 of the badge's 130 rather than the 62 the shared rule sets. */
  .case-challenge-tile img {
    width: 88px;
    height: 88px;
  }

  /* The plate's own proportions, so it fills without being cropped. */
  .case-solution-tile {
    aspect-ratio: 1389 / 875;
    margin-top: 90px;
  }

  .outcomes-3 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }

  /* ---- Tablet and mobile ---- */
  @media (max-width: 1180px) {
    .challenge,
    .solution,
    .results {
      min-height: 0;
    }

    /* Nothing is pinned here, so the tile can follow the plate exactly. */
    .case-hero-tile {
      height: auto;
      aspect-ratio: 1628 / 1638;
    }

    /* The desktop lead on the solution tile outranks the shared rule, so the
       halved and stacked layouts each restate it. */
    .case-solution-tile {
      margin-top: 64px;
    }

    .feature-label,
    .feature-one {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }

    /* With the label folded into its heading, the block carries the gap that
       used to sit above the label. */
    .feature-one {
      margin-top: 64px;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .feature-one {
      margin-top: 120px;
    }

    .case-solution-tile {
      margin-top: 48px;
    }
  }
</style>
